{
  "B_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u)*(sqrt((1+u)**2 + r)+(1+u))**2)",
  "C_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u))",
  "derivation": "Retained from draft. Let f=1+u=z and S=sqrt(f*f+r)=sqrt(p). Inside the region where chi_r=chi_u=1, choose the positive square root so that z+g=S, giving exact zero signed residual. The host form is g=r/2+r*r*B+u*r*C. With C=-1/(2*f), r/2+u*r*C=r/2-u*r/(2*f)=r*(f-u)/(2*f)=r/(2*f). With B=-1/(2*f*(S+f)**2), r*r*B=-r*r/(2*f*(S+f)**2). Therefore g=r/(2*f)-r*r/(2*f*(S+f)**2)=r*((S+f)**2-r)/(2*f*(S+f)**2). Since (S+f)**2-r=2*f*(S+f), this is r/(S+f)=S-f. Thus z_after=f+g=S and (z_after)**2=p. Smoothness: on the closed rectangle |r|<=1/2, |u|<=1/4, f>=3/4 and f*f+r>=1/16>0, so sqrt and the denominators are positive and smooth on an open neighborhood. This is a local coefficient-family cancellation, not a full NS certificate.",
  "inspect": [],
  "note": "Successor should retain this program. It cancels the full signed residual on the full chi support, not only the pure u=0 remainder. Outside the support the host still uses the linear r/2 reference; rows with |u|>1/4 or |r|>1/2 can show large residuals, which is expected and not a defect. Partial chi transition rows need not have zero residual. The returned certificate is a composition/domain certificate for the coefficient family only; finite execution and local exactness do not prove the full Navier-Stokes theorem."
}